WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.488
Book Four, Proposition Sixteen.

2
00:00:02.488 --> 00:00:09.294
In a given circle to inscribe a fifteen-angled figure which shall be both equilateral and equiangular.

3
00:00:09.294 --> 00:00:19.570
Let A B C D be the given circle; it is required to inscribe in the circle A B C D a fifteen-angled figure which shall be both equilateral and equiangular.

4
00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:33.812
In the circle A B C D, from vertex A, inscribe a side A C of the equilateral triangle inscribed in it by Four Two, and a side A B of an equilateral pentagon by Four Eleven.

5
00:00:33.812 --> 00:00:40.080
Then bisect the arc B C at E by Three Thirty, and join the chords B E and E C.

6
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.701
Suppose the circumference of the circle A B C D divided into fifteen equal parts.

7
00:00:45.701 --> 00:01:00.596
Because the equilateral triangle divides the circle into thirds, the arc A B C contains five of those fifteen parts; and because the regular pentagon divides the circle into fifths, the arc A B contains three of those fifteen parts.

8
00:01:00.596 --> 00:01:04.834
Therefore the remainder, arc B C, contains two of the equal parts.

9
00:01:04.834 --> 00:01:11.126
Since arc B C is bisected at E, each of the arcs B E and E C is one-fifteenth of the whole circle.

10
00:01:11.126 --> 00:01:24.032
If therefore we join B E and E C and fit straight lines equal to them continuously around the circle A B C D, a fifteen-angled figure which is both equilateral and equiangular has been inscribed in it.

11
00:01:24.032 --> 00:01:25.380
Which was to be done.

12
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:36.263
Proposition Four Sixteen crowns Book Four by combining the equilateral triangle and the golden-ratio pentagon through the harmonic identity one-third minus one-fifth equals two-fifteenths.

13
00:01:36.263 --> 00:01:48.229
As Proclus records, ancient astronomers prized the fifteen-angled figure because its central angle of twenty-four degrees measured the obliquity of the ecliptic—the tilt between the celestial equator and the zodiac.

14
00:01:48.229 --> 00:02:12.440
Together with continual arc bisection, Euclid's constructions of the three-gon, four-gon, five-gon, and fifteen-gon exhausted all known constructible regular polygons for over two millennia, until Carl Friedrich Gauss proved in seventeen ninety-six that a regular n-gon is constructible by straightedge and compass if and only if its odd prime factors are distinct Fermat primes—beginning with three and five, whose product is fifteen.

15
00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:27.513
